Streaming for the Anthropic provider, Open Agent Spec CLI 1.6.0. The provider
posts a message request and waits for the whole generation to come back as one
body, and through 1.6.2 there is no option to do otherwise. A long generation
delivered that way can be lost in transit. This streams instead, and
reassembles the events into the response dict the non-streaming path already
returns, so nothing downstream changes shape.

WHAT WENT WRONG. On 23 Aug 2026 four consecutive `oa run --task assess` calls
on the incident commander died with `PROVIDER_ERROR: Remote end closed
connection without response`, each after minutes of waiting, on a request the
same call had completed twice earlier the same day. Removing the
prompt-caching patch changed nothing. The identical request with
`stream: true`, built from the spec's own prompts and config, returned a
complete, contract-valid board in 79 seconds. So the model produces the board
and the contract accepts it; what fails is the delivery, and it fails in the
way that costs most: minutes of wall clock, then nothing, and an exit status
indistinguishable from a real failure. The commander's assess is the longest
generation in this repository and the most exposed.

Apply after the other two patches. All three are cut against the 1.6.0 wheel
in this order and apply with no fuzz. Resolve the file through the interpreter
`oa` runs on, not through system python3: a pipx install keeps `oas_cli` in its
own virtualenv, so python3 either cannot import it or finds a different copy
and patches a provider the demos never load.

    OA_PY="$(sed -n '1s|^#! *||p' "$(command -v oa)")"
    P="$("$OA_PY" -c 'import oas_cli.providers.anthropic_http as m; print(m.__file__)')"
    patch "$P" < patches/oas-1.6.0-anthropic-tool-messages.diff
    patch "$P" < patches/oas-1.6.0-anthropic-prompt-caching.diff
    patch "$P" < patches/oas-1.6.0-anthropic-streaming.diff

Set `intelligence.config.stream: false` in a spec to switch it off.

BOTH CALL SITES, NOT ONE. `invoke_verbose` serves the single-shot tasks and
`invoke_with_tools` serves every round of a tool loop, including the final
reply that carries the whole output. Patching only the first leaves the
Precedent Analyst's recall and the commander's tool-bound tasks on the old
failure mode, so the two share one `_post` helper and the streaming decision is
read from config in both. `checks/stream_probe.py` checks that by inspection,
the same way `checks/cache_probe.py` checks the caching call sites, because it
is the part a live call cannot see: a request that streams correctly reports
nothing about the request that did not.

WHAT REASSEMBLY HAS TO GET RIGHT. Text deltas concatenate in block order, and
thinking and signature deltas do the same so an extended-thinking response
still arrives with its blocks intact for `_extract_text_blocks` to skip. Tool
arguments arrive as `input_json_delta` fragments that are only valid JSON once
joined, and a tool call with no arguments sends no fragment at all, so an empty
accumulator has to mean `{}` rather than a parse failure. `stop_reason` comes
from `message_delta`, not `message_start`, which is what the tool loop branches
on. Usage is merged from both: `message_start` carries the input counts and the
cache fields, `message_delta` carries the real output count, and the cache
fields have to survive or this patch would quietly undo the reporting the
prompt-caching patch exists for.

WHERE IT FAILS CLOSED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH READING. A cut stream has a
loud form and a quiet one. Loud is no content at all. Quiet is every block
present, a plausible response shape, and `stop_reason` still None from
`message_start` because `message_delta` never landed, which
`invoke_with_tools` reads as "not a tool call" and hands back as a final
answer: a partial board, into a runner that exits zero on output it cannot
parse. `message_stop` is the last event of a finished stream, so its absence
catches both, and an empty content list is checked after it rather than
instead of it.

Two more faults get the same treatment for the same reason, that a response
which still has its blocks and still passes every shape check is the worst
thing this can return. A `data:` line that will not parse is text missing from
the middle of a generation, not noise to step over, and bytes that will not
decode are refused rather than replaced: the non-streaming path decodes
strictly and fails, so substituting U+FFFD would put a plausible altered
string inside a message that then parses cleanly, which is corruption
surviving every check instead of tripping one. And a delta type this does
not know how to apply is content going on the floor: `citations_delta` is the
live example, and a message quietly missing its citations is not the message
the non-streaming path would have returned, which is the only claim this patch
makes. An unknown *block* type is a different matter and passes through whole,
exactly as it would unstreamed.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. It does not surface tokens as they arrive.
The runner consumes a whole response, and a progressive display would be a
change to the CLI rather than to the provider. It does not retry: a stream
that closes early fails, loudly, at the checks above. Retry belongs in the
caller, where a transport failure can be told apart from a contract
rejection, and this repository puts it in the demo drivers.

The timeout changes meaning, which is worth knowing rather than a caveat. It
was a bound on one silent wait for the entire generation; it is now a bound on
each socket read. A request that produces tokens steadily can therefore run
longer than `timeout` in total, which is the intent: the failure being fixed is
a long quiet wait, and the setting that used to cut it short was cutting short
good runs as well.

Verified 23 Aug 2026 against an install rebuilt from the 1.6.0 wheel through
all three patches. `checks/stream_probe.py` reassembles a canned event stream,
covering text, extended thinking, an argument-bearing tool call and an
argument-free one, and compares the result against the non-streaming shape;
both call sites reach `_post`. Live: see the note added to README's Known
limits for what was run.

